Subject: [PATCH] net notifications: keep the module loaded instead of referencing it

NetNotificationService::FirstAdded() took a reference to its own module
with get_module() so that it stayed loaded while it had listeners. It
runs inside the module's generic syscall (net_notifications_control()),
and the module's uninit waits in unregister_generic_syscall() for that
syscall to return - while put_module() holds the module lock. A listener
added during an unload therefore deadlocked the boot on an x86_64 VM:

  net_server (46): holds "modules rlock"; put_module(stack) ->
    notifications_std_ops(B_MODULE_UNINIT) -> unregister_generic_syscall,
    waiting for the syscall
  launch_daemon (59): in net_notifications_control -> FirstAdded ->
    get_module, waiting for "modules rlock"; holds the "network" lock
  power_daemon, usb explore, uname: waiting behind them

The module is B_KEEP_LOADED now, so it is never uninitialized while the
syscall can run, and FirstAdded()/LastRemoved() no longer touch the
module lock at all.

diff --git a/src/add-ons/kernel/network/notifications/notifications.cpp b/src/add-ons/kernel/network/notifications/notifications.cpp
index cdb7609d55..486a6d3071 100644
--- a/src/add-ons/kernel/network/notifications/notifications.cpp
+++ b/src/add-ons/kernel/network/notifications/notifications.cpp
@@ -29,8 +29,6 @@ public:
 
 protected:
 	virtual	void				LastReferenceReleased();
-	virtual	void				FirstAdded();
-	virtual	void				LastRemoved();
 };
 
 
@@ -72,24 +70,6 @@ NetNotificationService::LastReferenceReleased()
 }
 
 
-void
-NetNotificationService::FirstAdded()
-{
-	// The reference counting doesn't work for us, as we'll have to
-	// ensure our module stays loaded.
-	module_info* dummy;
-	get_module(NET_NOTIFICATIONS_MODULE_NAME, &dummy);
-}
-
-
-void
-NetNotificationService::LastRemoved()
-{
-	// Give up the reference _AddListener()
-	put_module(NET_NOTIFICATIONS_MODULE_NAME);
-}
-
-
 //	#pragma mark - User generic syscall
 
 
@@ -169,7 +149,14 @@ notifications_std_ops(int32 op, ...)
 net_notifications_module_info sNotificationsModule = {
 	{
 		NET_NOTIFICATIONS_MODULE_NAME,
-		0,
+		// Never unloaded. It used to take a module reference while it had
+		// listeners, by calling get_module() from inside its own generic
+		// syscall. That deadlocked against an unload already in progress:
+		// put_module() held the module lock and waited in
+		// unregister_generic_syscall() for the syscall to return, while the
+		// syscall waited for the module lock (net_server and launch_daemon
+		// at boot).
+		B_KEEP_LOADED,
 		notifications_std_ops
 	},
 
